Subject: [PATCH 13] bpf: confine one ring-buffer reservation to its payload

Base: patches 0001-0004, 0006, 0008, 0010 and 0011. Protect one 31-byte
reservation per invocation, with reserve and submit/discard flags fixed to zero.
Keep the double-mapped storage parent and active reservation in trusted state.
Derive exact payload bounds from the actual reservation, and use the existing
object-relative access emitter and zero-permission RDDC. Storage authority is
optional at map creation; protected program admission requires it.

The Executive gateway matches the supplied release capability exactly against
its private active copy, then commits only the recorded address. Rejected
release arguments discard only that recorded reservation. No unchecked BPF
address is converted to a header pointer. Existing verifier reference rules
remain necessary; clearing the private slot does not revoke aliases.

Synchronous BPF re-entry from these gateways is unsupported. Assert that
restricted stack/data system registers are unchanged before returning to BPF;
do not attempt to restore or support a nested sandbox.

